  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a temperature-switchable polymer surface that controls how biomolecules and cells interact with a substrate. In both examples, the material is shown as a brush-like layer inside a recessed surface and can reversibly change between a hydrated, swollen state at about 20 °C and a collapsed, denser state at 37 °C.\n\nThe top row depicts a surface associated with BSA. At lower temperature, water is present within the expanded polymer layer, and the protein sits near the surface. When the temperature increases, the polymer chains collapse and the network appears more compact, with stronger internal hydrogen-bonding interactions and reduced water content.\n\nThe bottom row shows the same general mechanism but with an RGD-containing component, a common cell-adhesion motif. In the cool, hydrated state, the RGD group is displayed near the top of the polymer layer where it can contact a cell. In the warmer, collapsed state, the ligand appears buried or less accessible within the shrunken layer, and the cell is shown detached above the surface.\n\nOverall, the image conveys thermoresponsive control of protein adsorption and cell adhesion by reversible polymer hydration and chain conformation.",
